Status: IN PROGRESS
Milestone: Genuine cartridge-clock acceleration at 2x, 3x and 4x.
Dependencies: Completed workstreams 12, 19 and 23.

Purpose
-------
Fix native pacing conflicts that leave the fast-forward flag active without
accelerating the game. Verify actual emulated VBlanks, not just UI/API state.

Tasks
-----
[x] Inspect pinned speed settings and measure current public native loop progress.
[x] Reproduce native VSync overriding fast-forward pacing without changing its flag.
[x] Demonstrate the failure using an original test cartridge's own VBlank clock.
[x] Restore the requested native pacing without changing saved VSync preferences.
[x] Verify all speeds, return to 1x, runtime identity and existing audio protections.
[ ] Pass ordinary release gates and independently verify the public deployment.
[ ] Update affected guides, causal ledger and owned context.

Acceptance criteria
-------------------
Reselecting fast-forward after native VSync changes accelerates the actual
cartridge. All three requested ratios advance the original cartridge clock
proportionally on the qualified test system. Returning to 1x restores preferred
VSync. No core restart, save deletion, shader substitution or removed audio guard.
Default bundled Pixel Lab bytes are unchanged; only the test variant has a clock.
Record device-dependent throughput separately from functional timing correctness.

Completion evidence
-------------------
Initial public Edge measurements: original demo native-loop progression is
about 60/s at 1x and 174-239/s at 4x depending on room rendering/load.
The native VSync API reproducibly returns it to about 55-60/s while the
fast-forward flag and 4x label remain active. Reselecting 4x does not recover.
These initial values count native loop iterations, not independently verified
cartridge frames. The new test-only original VBlank clock will qualify that.
The real-cartridge regression fails before the fix: requested 4x advances
the SRAM VBlank clock at just 1.016x baseline after native VSync is enabled.
With explicit native VSync coordination, all 2x/3x/4x clock checks, return
to 1x, saved enabled/disabled VSync choices and unchanged runtime pass in21.8s.
Default and tone-only demo bytes independently match their original generator.
An existing audio case twice times out before power-on: trace shows ROM input
occurred before the scene/app was ready, leaving no selected cartridge.
Added an explicit existing scene-ready precondition, without weakening its
audio assertions or deadline. Final four-case Edge run passes1.4m.
Final shader-independent clock case passes Edge27.0s: baseline59.81 frames/s,
2x119.49, 3x179.89, 4x243.12 and restored1x60.33.
Explicit SwiftShader passes the other three cases, then the isolated clock
case58.6s with unchanged throughput assertions. The CRT/software baseline
was31.68 frames/s, so this clock test explicitly selects unfiltered output
and opens the tools drawer to isolate native pacing from GPU shader/room cost.
No product shader/scene/audio behavior, gate or deadline was changed.
Hosted release and independent public verification are pending.

Completion log
--------------
2026-11-04: User reports fast-forward is not accelerating emulation. Current
published original demo does accelerate initially; did not blame caching or
claim the user's specific cartridge/browser was reproduced.
2026-11-04: Isolated a real native VSync/fast-forward conflict. Added a focused
original-cartridge clock regression before changing production pacing.
